rminate. The data phase assigns the first insertion value to t0, and the link phase points t0's next field at the existing head i0. The function then returns t0, and the caller head becomes t0, so the list now begins with the newly inserted node followed by i0."},{"anchor":"synthetic_course_01:second_insertion","kind":"text","markdown":"On the second call, a fresh node t1 is allocated with indeterminate fields. Its data field is set to the second insertion value, and its next field is linked to the current head t0. The function returns t1, and the caller head is updated to t1, placing the second inserted node at the front of the list."},{"anchor":"synthetic_course_01:final_observation","kind":"text","markdown":"The final caller head is the most recently allocated node. Following the known links from that head yields the value prefix 2, then 1, then 9, before the traversal reaches a null pointer. Because the traversal terminates rather than repeating or becoming indeterminate, the status is terminated, and no allocation attempts failed. The reported prefix is the known value prefix, not a claim about any further or exact full traversal."},{"anchor":"synthetic_course_01:code","kind":"code","text":"#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct Node { int data; struct Node *next; } Node;
Node *insert(Node *head, int value, int allocation_success) {
